@namespace NoBorders.Components.Screens
@* Phase 7.0 (MIGRATION_PLAN.md) — the permanent BlazorWebView's actual root
   component, replacing the ad hoc `RootComponents.Add<SpecificScreen>("#app")`
   every prior phase used for isolated testing (MainWindowDisplayTab/
   MainWindowMatchingTab/SettingsView/ActivityLogWindow all still exist and
   still work standalone for that — this doesn't replace them, it composes
   them for the live app).

   Owns "which screen/tab/section is showing" as plain local state — pure
   Blazor-side UI presentation, no WinForms bridge needed (there is no real
   WinForms equivalent of "which screen is showing" the way _selectedGame or
   _activeScope have real MainForm fields behind them), same category as
   MainShell's own _showAddModal (Phase 4.8). No wrapping element around the
   @switch: MainShell/SettingsView/ActivityLogWindow's own root divs all rely
   on `height:100%` resolving against #app's own `height:100%` (wwwroot/css/
   type.css) — same direct-child relationship every single-screen root
   component already had, so an extra wrapper here would break that chain
   without adding anything.

   Deliberately NOT a DI service: there's only ever one AppShell instance
   (the whole app's root), so there's no cross-component sharing problem an
   injectable service would solve — it would just be indirection with no
   payoff, the same reasoning that's kept every other purely-local Phase 4/6/8
   modal-open flag (_showAddModal, _showArtworkModal) as component state
   instead of a service.

   Phase 8.4 bugfix: now also subscribes to AppStateService.Changed purely to
   notice when the *selected game* changes (by reference — editing the
   current game's own fields, e.g. Save Changes, mutates the same GameConfig
   object and correctly does NOT count) and reset _mainTab back to Display
   when it does. Confirmed live: switching games while the Matching tab was
   showing left the newly-selected game stuck on Matching too, since nothing
   here previously reacted to selection changing at all — _mainTab was only
   ever touched by the user's own tab clicks. *@

@using NoBorders.Services
@inject AppStateService State
@implements IDisposable

@switch (_screen)
{
    case Screen.Main:
        <MainShell ActiveTab="_mainTab" OnOpenSettings="OpenSettings" OnTabChange="SetMainTab">
            <RightPane>
                @if (_mainTab == MainShell.ShellTab.Display)
                {
                    <DisplayDetailPane />
                }
                else
                {
                    <MatchingDetailPane />
                }
            </RightPane>
        </MainShell>
        break;

    case Screen.Settings:
        <SettingsView Section="_settingsSection" OnBack="BackToMain" OnSectionChange="SetSettingsSection" OnOpenLog="OpenActivityLog" />
        break;

    case Screen.ActivityLog:
        <ActivityLogWindow OnBack="BackToSettings" />
        break;
}

@code {
    private enum Screen { Main, Settings, ActivityLog }

    private Screen _screen = Screen.Main;
    private MainShell.ShellTab _mainTab = MainShell.ShellTab.Display;
    private SettingsShell.SettingsSection _settingsSection = SettingsShell.SettingsSection.Monitors;
    private GameConfig? _lastSelectedGame;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _lastSelectedGame = State.SelectedGame;
        State.Changed += OnStateChanged;
    }

    public void Dispose() => State.Changed -= OnStateChanged;

    // Changed fires for lots of reasons that have nothing to do with which
    // game is selected (e.g. Save Changes editing the current game's own
    // fields) — reference equality against the last-seen SelectedGame is
    // what isolates "the selection itself actually changed" from those,
    // since switching selection always reassigns _selectedGame to a
    // different GameConfig object, while editing one in place never does.
    private void OnStateChanged()
    {
        if (!ReferenceEquals(State.SelectedGame, _lastSelectedGame))
        {
            _lastSelectedGame = State.SelectedGame;
            _mainTab = MainShell.ShellTab.Display;
        }
        InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    private void OpenSettings() => _screen = Screen.Settings;
    private void OpenActivityLog() => _screen = Screen.ActivityLog;

    // Back from Settings always returns to Main, remembering whichever main
    // tab (Display/Matching) was last active — _mainTab is only ever changed
    // by SetMainTab below, so it's untouched by the Settings/ActivityLog
    // detour.
    private void BackToMain() => _screen = Screen.Main;

    // Back from the Activity Log returns to Settings > Diagnostics
    // specifically, matching where "Open Log" always opens it from
    // (Adaptation Decision #2) rather than wherever Settings happened to be
    // showing before.
    private void BackToSettings()
    {
        _settingsSection = SettingsShell.SettingsSection.Diagnostics;
        _screen = Screen.Settings;
    }

    private void SetMainTab(MainShell.ShellTab tab) => _mainTab = tab;
    private void SetSettingsSection(SettingsShell.SettingsSection section) => _settingsSection = section;
}
